  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nA weak trough with mid level moisture and weak elevated instability \nwill exit by about early evening, which will end the showers and \nthunderstorms across the area. Behind this wave skies will clear a \nbit and with light wind some fairly chilly morning lows are \nexpected. Expect lows in most locations from the upper 40s to mid \n50s. With lighter winds and clear skies some patchy low land fog \nwill be possible.\n\nLow pressure moves along the Canadian border on Thursday, with a \ntrailing trough expected to move through the area Thursday night. \nFor now the atmosphere looks a little too dry for much in the way of \nshowers or thunderstorms, so will keep chances at 20 percent or less.\n\nFriday is a tough call as instability increases across the area, \nbut capping looks to limit the convective potential. Also of note is \nsome disagreement on timing of the weak wave moving through Thursday \nnight. If the faster runs are more accurate, then capping is likely \nto be even more difficult to overcome. A slower trough could allow \nfor a little additional heating which would support a few \nthunderstorms.\n\nModels continue to point towards faster westerly flow aloft Saturday \nand Sunday which may bring some scattered showers and thunderstorms \nto the area this weekend. Currently looking like Sunday night will \nbe the better chance for showers and storms.\n\nUpper level flow will turn light northerly early next week which \nwill bring temperatures back to below normal with limited chances \nfor precipitation.",
